      <![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><h3>Taiwan Strait shipping and the insurance question</h3><div>The Strategic Brief (newsletter), June 2026</div><div><br/></div><div>Most discussion of a crisis in the Taiwan Strait focuses on missiles and fleets. This issue looks at something duller and possibly more decisive: marine insurance.</div><div><br/></div><div>A large share of global container traffic and much of the world's advanced chip output pass near the strait. Commercial shipping does not need a blockade to stop; it needs underwriters to raise war-risk premiums high enough, or to withdraw cover, and shipowners will reroute or wait. That decision is made in a handful of insurance markets, quickly, and without any government declaring anything.</div><div><br/></div><div>The implication is that "gray-zone" pressure (inspections, exercises that close sea lanes for days, coast-guard incidents) can impose real economic costs while staying below the threshold of war. Planners who model only military thresholds miss the commercial ones, which come first.</div></en-note>]]>
    </content>
  </note>
</en-export>
